{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in the first ~5–10 days but become increasingly too low beyond ~day 20, especially in rising markets (late-horizon underestimation of rallies).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when prices break out upward, the forecast follows the direction but with insufficient slope (misses acceleration), leading to growing negative error into weeks 3–4.",
    "Over-mean-reversion in drawdowns: during mid-horizon dips (around days ~12–20 in examples), the model tends to project too deep/too persistent weakness versus realized prices (downside overshoot).",
    "Spike handling: after strong upside moves, the model often caps/plateaus rather than extending the move; apply an upward adjustment when recent momentum is positive and volatility has expanded.",
    "Practical heuristic: apply a linear ramp-up correction that increases with horizon (near-zero at day 1, rising to +3 to +7 EUR by days 25–30) when the last 10–15 days show a positive trend; apply a smaller upward correction (+1 to +3 EUR) in mid-horizon when the model projects a sharp dip but spot trend remains flat-to-up."
  ]
}
